If the bundle is trivialized by the foliated flow, the restriction of leafwise differential forms to the fiber $S$ induces the homomorphism
$$ \Psi : \bar{H}_{\cal F}^n(M) \longrightarrow H^n(S, \mathbb{R})\otimes_{\mathbb{R}} C^{\infty}(S^1); \;\; [\omega] \mapsto [\omega|_S] \otimes 1,$$
which is an isomorphism on the image ${\rm Im}(\Psi)$. Here the operator $\Theta_n$ corresponds to $\displaystyle{ {\rm id}_{H^n(S,\mathbb{R})} \otimes \frac{d}{dt}. }$ 
\\

It remains to show that (A4) is . Let $g_{\cal F}$ be the restriction of the Riemannian metric $g$ to the leaves of ${\cal F}$. The orthogonal complement $T({\cal F})^{\bot} \subset T(M)$ and the volume form ${\rm vol}_M$ are invariant by the vertical flow $\psi$. The space ${\cal A}^\bullet_{\cal F}(M)$ can be considered as the subspace ${\cal A}^{0,\bullet}(M)$ of ${\cal A}^\bullet(M)$ consisting of the forms that vanich if some vector is orthogonal to the leaves. Since the orthogonal projection $\pi_{0, \bullet} : {\cal A}^{\bullet}(M) \to {\cal A}^{0,\bullet}(M) \equiv {\cal A}_{\cal F}^{\bullet}(M)$ is determined by $T({\cal F})^{\bot}$, it follows that
$$ \psi^{t *} \circ \pi_{0, \bullet} = \pi_{0,\bullet} \circ {\psi^{t *}$$
on ${\cal A}^{\bullet}(M)$. So ${\psi^{t *}$  on ${\cal A}^\bullet(M)$ preserves the subspace ${\cal A}^{0,\bullet}(M) \equiv {\cal A}_{\cal F}^{\bullet}(M)$, where it agrees with the action $\psi^{t*}$ on ${\cal A}^\bullet_{\cal F}(M)$ used before. We also have
$$ \psi^{t *} \circ d_{\cal F} = d_{\cal F} \circ \psi^{t *}$$
on ${\cal A}^\bullet({\cal F})$. Using that $g_{\cal F}$ is invariant by $\psi^t$, we also get
$$ \psi^{t *} \circ d_{\cal F}^* = d_{\cal F}^* \circ \psi^{t *}$$
on ${\cal A}_{\cal F}^{\bullet}(M)$. So we have
$$ \psi^{t *} \circ \Delta_{\cal F} = \Delta_{\cal F} \circ \psi^{t *}$$
on ${\cal A}_{\cal F}^{\bullet}(M)$, obtaining that $\psi^{t *}$ preserves ${\rm Ker}(\Delta_{\cal F})$ and
$$ \psi^{t *} \circ \Pi_{\cal F} = \Pi_{\cal F} \circ \psi^{t *},$$
where $\Pi_{\cal F} : {\cal A}_{\cal F}^{\bullet}(M) \to {\rm Ker}(\Delta_{\cal F})$ is the orthogonal projection (Theorem 1.7). Then, using the isomorphism $\bar{H}_{\cal F}^{\bullet}(M) \simeq {\rm Ker}(\Delta_{\cal F})$ in Theorem 1.7, we get that the homomorphism $\psi^{t *}$ on $\bar{H}^{\buleet}_{\cal F}(M)$ corresponds to the homomorphism $\Pi_{\cal F} \circ \psi^{t *} = \psi^{t *}$ on ${\rm Ker}(\Delta_{\cal F})$, where now $\psi^{t *}$ is the restriction to ${\rm Ker}(\Delta_{\cal F})$ of ${\psi^t}^*$ on ${\cal A}_{\cal F}^{\bullet}(M)$. Hence the Lie derivative ${\cal L}_X$  on ${\cal A}^\bullet(M)$ preserves the subspaces ${\cal A}^{0,\bullet}(M)\equiv {\cal A}^\bullet_{\cal F}(M)$ and ${\rm Ker}(\Delta_{\cal F})$, and $\Theta_{\bullet}$ on $\bar{H}_{\cal F}^{\bullet}(M)$ corresponds to the restriction of ${\cal L}_X$ to ${\cal A}_{\cal F}^{\bullet}(M)$. Now, consider leafwise differential forms with complex coefficients 
${\cal A}_{\cal F}^{\bullet}(M)_{\mathbb{C}} = {\cal A}_{\cal F}^{\bullet}(M) \otimes \mathbb{C}$, and the extension of $g_{\cal F}$ to a Hermitian metric on  ${\cal A}_{\cal F}^{\bullet}(M)_{\mathbb{C}}$. Let $\alpha \in {\rm Ker}(\Delta_{\cal F}) \simeq \bar{H}_{\cal F}^{\bullet}(M)_{\mathbb{C}}$ be a non-zero eigenvector of $\Theta_{\bullet} = {\cal L}_X$ with eigenvalue $\rho \in \mathbb{C}$. Since ${\cal L}_X g_{\cal F} = 0$ and ${\cal L}_X {\rm vol}_M = 0$, we have
$$\begin{array}{ll} 
{\cal L}_X(|\alpha|^2 {\rm vol}_M) & = (g_{\cal F}({\cal L}_X \alpha, \alpha) + g_{\cal F}(\alpha, {\cal L}_X \alpha)) {\rm vol}_M\\
                                  & = (g_{\cal F}(\rho \alpha, \alpha) + g_{\cal F}(\alpha, \rho \alpha)) {\rm vol}_M \\
                                  & = (\rho |\alpha|^2 + \bar{\rho} |\alpha|^2) {\rm vol}_M\\
                                  & = 2 {\rm Re}(\rho) |\alpha|^2 {\rm vol}_M.
 \end{array}$$ 
Then we have 
$$ \begin{array}{ll} 
 0 & = \displaystyle{ \int_M {\cal L}_X(|\alpha|^2 {\rm vol}_M) } \\
   & = \displaystyle{ 2 {\rm Re}(\rho) \int_M |\alpha|^2 {\rm vol}_M }\\
   & = 2 {\rm Re}(\rho) ||\alpha||^2.
\end{array}$$
Therefore ${\rm Re}(\rho) = 0$ because $\alpha \neq 0$. Hence (A4) is satisfied.


ÉAÉuÉXÉgÉâÉNÉg:
We prove a regularized determinant expressions of the dynamical zeta function, which was conjectured by Deninger, for certain 3-dimensional Riemannian foliated dynamical systems.  The proof is based on computing the dynamical $\xi$-function by the distributional dynamical Lefschetz trace formula. 


ÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñ ExamplesÅ@ÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñ

Consider a suspension foliation ${\cal F}$ on a suspension manifold $M = \widetilde{L} \times_{\mathbb{Z}} \mathbb{R}$, where $\widetilde{L}$ is a $\mathbb{Z}$-covering of a compact manifold $L$. The leaves of ${\cal F}$ are the projections of the horizontal fibers $\widetilde{L} \times \{t\}$. Take any Riemannian metric on $L$ and consider its lift to the leaves of ${\cal F}$. Take the vertical vector field $X = \partial_t$ on $M$ generates a foliated flow $\phi$ which is isometric on the leaves of ${\cal F$. Now take any vector field $Y$ in $L$ whose flow has simple fixed points and no closed orbits. Let $Y$ also denote the lift of $Y$ to the leaves of ${\cal F}$. Then $Z = X+Y$ generates a foliated flow $\psi$ with simple closed orbits. By Lemma 5.1, there is a leafwise homotopy between $\phi$ and $\psi$. \\
\\
{\bf Example 5.3.} $T^3 = R^3/Z^3$ with a foliation F of codimension 1 whose leaves are projections of parallel planes in R^3. Consider also the flat bundle-like metric g on T^3 obtained as the projection of the Riemannian metric on R^3. If the slopes of the parallel planes in R^3 along the x-axis and y-axis are irrational numbers, then the leaves of F are dense. The flow \phi^t on T^3 defined by the unit vector field on R^3 perpendicular to the parallel planes is foliated and acts isometrically on the leaves of F. This \phi^t is leafwise-homotopic to a simple foliated flow.

You can also take the full-back of F by any smooth map M \to T^3 transverse to F, where M is a closed manifold; this means adding handles to the leaves along closed transversals and getting infinite-dimensional leafwise reduced cohomology. Any Riemannian foliation of codimension one with dense leaves on a closed manifold is such a pull-back. The \phi^t on T^3 also corresponds to a foliated flow for the pull-back foliation acting isometrically on the leaves for some metric and leafwise homotopic to a simple foliated flow. 

The number of closed orbits of these simple foliated flows must be at most countable. In the case of handles in the leaves, there must be simple closed orbits. In the case of plane leaves, there may not be any closed orbit.\\
\\
Let \Sigma_2 be the closed oriented surface of genus 2. One can take a homeomorphism h: \pi_1(\Sigma_2) to S^1 \equiv R/Z, whose image is a dense subgroup of S^1 with 2 generators. The corresponding suspension foliation F has dense leaves, which are covers of \Sigma_2, and all leaves diffeomorphic one another. I think F satisfies all conditions you mention.

More generally, one can consider the closed oriented surface \Sigmag of genus g and a homomorphism h: \pi(\Sigma_g) \to S^1 whose image is a dense subgroup of rank g. The corresponding suspension has dense leaves and all propeties you mentioned.\\
\\
Yes, I think that \psi^t satisfies (9). There is a flow \bat\psi^t on \Sigma_g with simple fixed points such, for every point x in a closed orbit \gamma of \psi^t, the tangent map \psi^{k \ell(\gamma)}_* : T_x F \to T_x F corresponds to  \bar\psi^{k \ell(\gamma)}_* : T_{\bar x} \Sigma_g  \to T_{\bar x} \Sigma_g for some fixed point \bar x of \bar\psi^t. The map \bar\psi^t_* : T_{\bar x} \Sigma_g  \to T_{\bar x} \Sigma_g satisfies that the sign of the determinant of id - \bar\psi^t_* depends continuously on t, and therefore is constant because it takes values +1 or -1.

You're right. But the vertical foliated flow of the suspension foliation is not simple. You have to take a leafwise homotopic foliated flow which is simple. This new simple foliated flow has a countable set of periodic orbits and defines the same action on the leafwise reduced cohomology.
\\
\\
{\bf Example 5.4.} Take any suspension foliated manifold (M,F). Its leaves are transverse to the fibers of a fiber bundle \pi:M \to B. Take also a bundle-like metric on M so that \pi is a Riemannian submersion for some Riemannian metric on B. Then we can consider the vertical flow \phi^t, which acts isometrically on the leafwise differential forms. Let X denote the infinitesimal generator of \phi^t. In the base manifold B, take the gradient vector field \bar Y of a Morse function. Let Y be the horizontal lift of \bar Y to M; thus Y is tangent to the leaves. Let \psi^t denote the foliated flow on (M,F) induced by the vector field Z=X+Y. This \psi^t is simple, leafwise homotopic to \phi^t, and its closed orbits correspond to the zero points of \bar Y. So the periods of the closed orbits of \psi^t are the integers, and, for every integer k, the number of closed orbits of period k is equal to the number of zero points of \bar Y. So the zeta function Z(s) is defined in this case for s \gg 0.

We need a homomorphism h: \pi_1(B) \to R. Let \widetilde B be the universal covering of B. Consider the canonical right action of \pi_1(B) on \widetilde B. Then M is the quotient of \widetilde M := \widetilde B \times R by the diagonal action of \pi_1(B) given by (\tilde x, r)\cdot \alpha = (\tilde x \cdot \alpha, r - h(\alpha)).
The horizontal foliation F on M is induced by the foliation \widetilde F on \widetilde M with leaves \widetilde B \times \{r\} (observe that \widetilde F is invariant by the action of \pi_1(B)). The projection \pi: M \to B is induced by the 1st factor projection \widetilde M \to \widetilde B and the covering projection \widetilde B \to B. The restriction of B to the leaves of F are coverings of B. The vertical flow \tilde\phi^t on \widetilde M is defined by \widetilde\phi^t(\tilde x, r) = (\tilde x, t+r), whose infinitesimal generator is \widetilde X = (0, \partial_r). It induces a vertical flow \phi^t on M given by \phi^t([\tilde x, r]) = [\tilde x, t+r], whose infinitesimal generator is denoted by X. The leaves of F are dense if and only if the image of h: \pi_1(B) \to R is dense in R.

Assume B is a closed oriented surface of genus at least 2. Then there are homomorphisms h with dense image and there are circles C embedded in B so that the restriction of F to \pi^{-1}(C) is a product foliation (take C defining an element of \pi_1(B) whose image by h is 0. Then M is compact, F has dense leaves, and the reduced leafwise cohomology of F is of infinite dimension.

Take a vector field \bar Y on B, and let Y denote the lift of \bar Y tangent to the leaves of F (the horizontal lift of \bar Y). Let Z = X+Y and let \psi^t denote the flow of Z, which is a foliated flow for F. Note that \psi^t may have closed orbits but there is no fixed point. If \bar Y is gradient-like for some Morse function on B, then the closed orbits of \psi^tare simple and have periods the integers. Moreover, for every integer k, the closed orbits of period k correspond bijectively to the zero points of \bar Y. If bar Y has N zero points and k is a positive integer, then the number of closed orbits of \psi^t with period in [1,k] is Nk. Thus Sunada's condition is satisfied in this case: \nu(x) = N\lfloor x \rfloor \le x/\log(x) for x\gg 0.\\


Let \bar\psi^t be the flow of \bar Y. Note that the projection \pi: M \to B is equivariant w.r.t. the flows \psi^t on M and \bar\psi^t on B.

Let \gamma be a closed orbit of period k and let x \in \gamma. By the equivariance, \gamma corresponds to a zero point \pi(x) of \bar Y, which is a fixed point of \bar\psi^t. Moreover the tangent map \psi^k_{* x} : T_xF \to T_x F corresponds to the tangent map \bar\psi^k_{* \pi(x)} : T_{\pi(x)}B \to T_{\pi(x)}B. So \epsilon(\gamma) is equal to the sign of the determinant of \bar\psi^k_{* \pi(x)} - id : T_{\pi(x)}B \to T_{\pi(x)}B. 

Since the map \bar\psi^t_{* \pi(x)} - id : T_{\pi(x)}B \to T_{\pi(x)}B is defined and non-singular for all t \ne 0, we get that \epsilon_\gamma depends only on the sign of k.

But \bar Y is a gradient-like vector field of a Morse function f on the surface B. Taking Morse coordinates, we have 3 local models:

Case of Morse index 0: 
f(x,y) = -x^2-y^2.
\bar Y = \nabla f = (-2x, -2y). 
\bar\psi^t(x,y) = (e^{-2t} x, e^{-2t) y).
\bar\psi^t_{* (0,0)} \equiv \begin{pmatrix} e^{-2t} & 0 \\ 0 & e^{-2t} \end{pmatrix}.
\bar\psi^t_{* (0,0)} -id \equiv \begin{pmatrix} e^{-2t} - 1 & 0 \\ 0 & e^{-2t} - 1 \end{pmatrix}, with positive determinant for all t \ne 0. 
Then \epsilon_\gamma = 1 for any possible period k of \gamma.

Case of Morse index 1: 
f(x,y) = x^2-y^2.
\bar Y = \nabla f = (2x, -2y). 
\bar\psi^t(x,y) = (e^{2t} x, e^{-2t) y).
\bar\psi^t_{* (0,0)} \equiv \begin{pmatrix} e^{2t} & 0 \\ 0 & e^{-2t} \end{pmatrix}.
\bar\psi^t_{* (0,0)} -id \equiv \begin{pmatrix} e^{2t} - 1 & 0 \\ 0 & e^{-2t} - 1 \end{pmatrix}, with negative determinant for all t \ne 0. 
Then \epsilon_\gamma = -1 for any possible period k of \gamma.

Case of Morse index 2: 
f(x,y) = x^2+y^2. 
\bar Y = \nabla f = (2x, 2y).
\bar\psi^t(x,y) = (e^{2t} x, e^{2t) y).
\bar\psi^t_{* (0,0)} \equiv \begin{pmatrix} e^{2t} & 0 \\ 0 & e^{2t} \end{pmatrix}.
\bar\psi^t_{* (0,0)} -id \equiv \begin{pmatrix} e^{2t} - 1 & 0 \\ 0 & e^{2t} - 1 \end{pmatrix}, with positive determinant for all t \ne 0. 
Then \epsilon_\gamma = 1 for any possible period k of \gamma.

So \epsilon_\gamma depends on the Morse index in the above way. We can group the closed orbits according to the Morse index, obtaining that the function Z(s) has 3 factors, 2 of them with exponent -1, and one of them with exponent 1.
ÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñÅñ


$\phi^t$ is isometric on the leaves of ${\cal F}$, and therefore the computations of you student can be applied to $\phi^t$.\\
There is a leafwise homotopy between $\phi^t$ and a foliated flow $\psi^t$ transverse to the leaves and with simple closed orbits. So the trace formula can be applied to $\psi^t$ in that case. But $\phi^t$ and $\psi^t$ induce the same action on the reduced leafwise cohomology because they are leafwise homotopic. So the trace formula for $\psi^t$ can be considered as a trace formula for $\phi^t$.\\
\\
For instance, consider a suspension foliation ${\cal F}$ on a suspension manifold $M = \widetilde{L} \times_{\mathbb{Z}} \mathbb{R}$, where $\widetilde{L}$ is a $\mathbb{Z}$-covering of a compact manifold $L$. The leaves of F are the projections of the horizontal fibers $\widetilde L \times \{t\}$. Take any Riemannian metric on $L$ and consider its lift to the leaves of ${\cal F}$. Take the vertical vector field $X = \partial_t$ on $M$ generates a foliated flow $\phi^t$ which is isometric on the leaves of ${\cal F}$. Now take any vector field Y in L whose flow has simple fixed points and no closed orbits. Let $Y$ also denote the lift of $Y$ to the leaves of ${\cal F}$. Then $Z = X+Y$ generates a foliated flow $\psi^t$ with simple closed orbits and there is a leafwise homotopy between $\phi^t$ and $\psi^t$.\\



