{\bf Remark 1.7.} As Deninger and Kopei suggested (cf. [D1] $\sim$ [D6], [Ko1], [Ko2]), an FDS$^3$ $\frak{S} = (M, {\cal F}, \phi)$  may be regarded as a geometric analogue of a compact arithmetic curve, namely, a smooth proper algebraic curve over a finite field or $\overline{{\rm Spec}({\cal O}_k)} = {\rm Spec}({\cal O}_k) \cup {\cal P}_k^{\infty}$ for the ring ${\cal O}_k$ of integers of a number field $k$, where ${\cal P}_{k}^{\infty}$ is the set of infinite primes of $k$. The set ${\cal P}_{\frak{S}}$ corresponds to the set of closed points (finite primes) of $C$ or ${\rm Spec}({\cal O}_k)$. When  ${\cal P}_{\frak{S}}^{\infty}$ is non-empty, it corresponds to ${\cal P}_k^{\infty}$.  So the analogy is closer if ${\cal P}_{\frak{S}}$ is a countably infinite set. We give such examples in the section 3. \\ 
\\
{\bf Definition 1.8.} Let $\frak{S} = (M, {\cal F}, \phi)$ and $\frak{S}' = (M', {\cal F}', \phi')$ be FDS$^3$'s. An {\em FDS$^3$-morphism} $\frak{S} \rightarrow \frak{S}'$ is defined by a morphism of foliated $3$-manifolds by Riemann surfaces (cf. Definition 1.3) such that  $f : M \rightarrow M'$ commutes with the flows, namely, $f \circ \phi^t = \phi'^t \circ f$ for any $t \in \mathbb{R}$.

An {\em FDS$^3$-isomorphism} $\frak{S} \stackrel{\sim}{\rightarrow} \frak{S}'$ is an FDS$^3$-morphism which is an isomorphism of foliated $3$-manifolds by Riemann surfaces.  We identify $\frak{S}$ with $\frak{S}'$ if there is an FDS$^3$-isomorphism between them.
\\
\\
 {\bf Definition 1.9.} Let $\frak{S} = (M, {\cal F}, \phi)$ be an FDS$^3$. An {\em FDS$^3$-meromorphic function} on $\frak{S}$ is defined to be a smooth map $f : M_0   \rightarrow \mathbb{P}^1(\mathbb{C}) = \mathbb{C} \cup \{ \infty\}$ satisfying the following conditions:\\
 (1) $f$ restricted to any leaf is a meromorphic function,\\
 (2) the zeros and poles of $f$ lie along finitely many closed orbits.\\
\\
For an FDS$^3$ $\frak{S} =(M, {\cal F}, \phi )$, let $T{\cal F}$ denote the subbundle of the tangent bundle $T M_0$  whose total space is the union of the tangent spaces of leaves, and let  $\dot{\phi}^t = \frac{d}{dt} \phi^t$ be the vector field on $M_0$ which generates the flow $\phi$.\\
\\
{\bf Lemma 1.10.} {\em For an FDS$^3$ $\frak{S} =(M, {\cal F}, \phi )$, there is the unique closed smooth $1$-form $\omega$ on $M_0$ satisfying}
$$ \omega|_{T{\cal F}} = 0, \;\; \;\;  \omega(\dot{\phi}^t) = 1. \leqno{({\rm C})}$$
{\em More precisely, let $(M,{\cal F},\phi)$ be a triple satisfying (1), (2), (3) and (i) in Definition 1.5. Then there is the unique smooth $1$-form $\omega$ on $M_0$ satisfying} (C), {\em and the condition} (ii) {\em is equivalent to that $\omega$ is closed.}\\
\\
{\em Proof.} Let $(M,{\cal F},\phi)$ be a triple satisfying (1), (2), (3) and (i) in Definition 1.5. Then we can take a smooth $1$-form $\omega$ on $M_0$ such that for each foliated coordinate
 $(z,t) \in U$, $\omega|_{U} = h(z,t)dt$. Writing $\dot{\phi}^t = a(z,t)\partial_z + b(z,t)\partial_{\overline{z}} + c(z,t)\partial_t$, we have $\omega(\dot{\phi}^t) = c(z,t)h(z,t)$ and so $h(z,t)$ is uniquely determined by $\omega(\dot{\phi}^t) = 1$. This yields the first assertion.

Now, assume the condition (ii). For each point $p \in M_0$, there is a foliated local coordinate $(z,t) \in U$ such that $\phi^s(z,t) = (z, t+s)$. So there is a system of foliated local coordinates $(U_i; \varphi_i)_{i \in I}$ of $M_0$ such that if $U_i \cap U_j$ is not empty,  $(\varphi_j \circ \varphi_i^{-1})(z_i,t_i) = (f_{ij}(z,t), t+c_{ij})$ for some constant $c_{ij} \in \mathbb{R}$. Hence $\omega|_{U_i} = dt_i$ and so $\omega$ is closed.

Conversely, assume that $d\omega = 0$. We may take each $U_i$ is simply connected so that $\omega|_{U_i} = dt_i$ by Poincar\'{e}'s Lemma. Then we can follow the above argument in the reverse way to obtain the condition (ii). $\;\; \Box$\\
\\
{\bf Definition 1.11.} Let $\frak{S} = (M, {\cal F}, \phi)$ be an FDS$^3$. We call the smooth closed $1$-form in Lemma 1.10 the {\em canonical $1$-form} of $\frak{S}$ and denote it by $\omega_{\frak{S}}$. The de Rham cohomology class of  $\omega_{\frak{S}}$ defines the {\em period homomorphism}  
$$ [\omega_{\frak{S}}] : H_1(M_0;\mathbb{Z}) \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}; \;\; [\ell] \mapsto \int_{\ell} \omega_{\frak{S}},$$
and the {\em period group} of $\frak{S}$ is defined by the image of  $[\omega_{\frak{S}}]$, which we denote by $\Lambda_{\frak{S}}$.\\
\\
The following lemma will be used in the examples of the section 3.\\
\\
{\bf Lemma 1.12.} {\em Let $\frak{S}_1 = (M, {\cal F}, \phi_1)$ and $\frak{S}_2 = (M, {\cal F}, \phi_2)$ be FDS$^3$'s. Then we have $\omega_{\frak{S}_1} = \omega_{\frak{S}_2}$ if and only if $(\dot{\phi}_1^t  - \dot{\phi}_2^t)_p \in T_p{\cal F}$ for any $p \in M_0$.}\\
\\
{\em Proof.} Since $\omega_{\frak{S}_i}|_{T{\cal F}} = 0, \omega_{\frak{S}_i}(\dot{\phi}_i^t) = 1$ for $i=1,2$, we have
$$\begin{array}{ll}    (\dot{\phi}_1^t  - \dot{\phi}_2^t)_p \in T_p{\cal F} \;\mbox{for any}\;  p \in M_0 & \Leftrightarrow \omega_{\frak{S}_1}(\dot{\phi}_1^t  - \dot{\phi}_2^t) = 0\\
                                                                                                                                                       &    \Leftrightarrow \omega_{\frak{S}_1}(\dot{\phi}_2^t) =  \omega_{\frak{S}_1}(\dot{\phi}_1^t) = 1\\
                                                                                                                                                       &  \Leftrightarrow \omega_{\frak{S}_1}|_{T{\cal F}} = 0, \omega_{\frak{S}_1}(\dot{\phi}_2^t) = 1\\
                                                                                                                                                       & \Leftrightarrow \omega_{\frak{S}_2} = \omega_{\frak{S}_1}.  $


ÉAÉuÉXÉgÉâÉNÉg:
We prove a regularized determinant expression of the dynamical zeta function, which was conjectured by Deninger, for certain 3-dimensional Riemannian foliated dynamical systems.  The proof is based on computing the dynamical $\xi$-function by the distributional dynamical Lefschetz trace formula. We start to explain the background and motivation of our work, which are coming from the cohomological study of the number theoretic zeta functions. This is the joint work of Jesus Antonio Alvarez Lopez and Junhyeong Kim. 

 